<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napoleon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Napoleon Bonaparte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napoleon Bonaparte was a French military leader who seized power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and ruled as Emperor of the French. His conquests reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: he dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, and his occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> helped trigger the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. His invasion of Portugal drove the Portuguese court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1808, a step towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" width="252" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the French Revolution the Republic refused in 1793 to accept the quèstia, rejecting the French co-sovereignty as a feudal relic, and for over a decade the bishop was the only co-prince until <b>Napoleon</b> restored the French rights in 1806 at the Andorrans' request.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Napoleon</b> had made himself Emperor of the French in May, and the elective Holy Roman crown was visibly failing after the territorial upheavals of the Imperial Recess of 1803, so Francis took a new hereditary title, Emperor of Austria, as Francis I. The title gave a common name…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Coastal towers and Fort Regent were built against <b>Napoleon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Napoleon</b>’s Act of Mediation of 1803 restored Zürich as a canton of the Swiss Confederation, now with rural representation, and the liberal movement launched at the Ustertag of 1830 brought equal rights for town and country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1807, fearing that <b>Napoleon</b> would seize the Danish navy, Britain bombarded Copenhagen and carried off the fleet, driving Denmark-Norway into alliance with France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The end came with the French Revolutionary Wars: after <b>Napoleon Bonaparte</b>’s victory at the Battle of Lodi he entered Milan on 15 May 1796.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ajaccio, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Longwood House. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.93,8.74&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Ajaccio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4762 671 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Napoleon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.93,8.74&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is my wish that my ashes may repose on the banks of the Seine, in the midst of the French people, whom I have loved so well.&#8221;</p><footer>His will, 1821 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon%27s_Addresses/Napoleon%27s_Will" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Soldiers of my old guard, I bid you farewell. For twenty years I have constantly accompanied you on the road to honor and glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell to the Old Guard, Fontainebleau, 1814 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon%27s_Addresses/Part_V" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My waking thoughts are all of thee. Your portrait and the remembrance of last night's delirium have robbed my senses of repose.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Josephine, 1796 &#183; tr. Henry Foljambe Hall, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37499/pg37499-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is on the field of battle, amid the sufferings of a multitude of wounded, and surrounded by fifteen thousand corpses, that I beseech your Majesty to listen to the voice of humanity…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Emperor of Austria from the field of Marengo, June 1800 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon's_Addresses/Part_III" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Among the Russians there are no generals in contending against whom I can acquire any glory. All I wish is to obtain the victory with the least possible bloodshed. My soldiers are my children.&#8221;</p><footer>Proclamation to the army after Ulm, October 1805 &#183; tr. in Napoleon's Addresses, ed. Ida M. Tarbell, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Napoleon's_Addresses/Part_IV" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But what a romance is my life!&#8221;</p><footer>Conversation at St Helena (Las Cases, Memorial of St Helena, 1823) &#183; English translation, London 1836 (translator not named) &#183; as recorded by his companion in exile, the Count de Las Cases &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/53968/pg53968-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
